WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.570
BRITTA DEGROOT: My
name is Britta DeGroot.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.630
And I'm a junior, studying
music with a concentration

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.430
in piano performance.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.300
It is an honor and a pleasure to
introduce the speaker tonight.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.660
John Steele Gordon was
educated at Millbrook School

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.820
and Vanderbilt University.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.040
His articles have appeared
in numerous publications,

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.620
including Forbes, National
Review, Commentary,

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.020
The New York Times, The
Wall Street Journal.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.700
He is the contributing
editor at American Heritage,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.470
where he wrote the
"Business of America"

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.920
column for many years and
currently writes "The Long

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.520
View" column for Barron's.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.400
He is the author
of several books,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.420
including Hamilton's Blessing,
The Extraordinary Life

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.350
and Times of Our National Debt;
The Great Game, the Emergence

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.070
of Wall Street as a World
Power; The Business of America;

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.740
Tales from the Marketplace,
American Enterprise

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.280
from the Settling of New
England to the Breakup of AT&amp;T;

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.460
and An Empire of Wealth,
the Epic History of American

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.020
Economic Power.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.740
Please join me in welcoming
Mr. Gordon to the podium.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:34.805 --> 00:01:36.180
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
Good evening.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.410
I am both happy and honored to
be back at Hillsdale College

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.630
once again.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:46.350
I'm here to talk about Socialism
in American history, which

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:52.230
is to say the history of
Socialism on very stony ground.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.020
Socialism is, by definition,
an economic system

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.740
wherein the means of production
are held in common, that is,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.600
by the government.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.410
Socialism, therefore,
necessarily concentrates power,

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.380
for while the people
might, in theory, own

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.760
the means of production, only
individuals can actually act.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.650
So Socialism in
all its many forms,

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.240
from the Democratic Socialism
of post-World War II Britain,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.870
to the unspeakable horrors
of Pol Pot's Cambodia,

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.050
to that weird combination,
a Communist monarchy

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:30.030
in North Korea, means not only
economic power to the people

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.120
but economic power
to fiduciaries

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:37.310
for the people, the same people
who exercise political power.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.180
And as Lord Acton
famously noted,

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.440
"Power corrupts, and absolute
power corrupts absolutely."

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.180
That's why, in
capitalist countries,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.860
the rich become powerful.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.912
In Socialist ones, the
powerful become rich.

00:02:49.912 --> 00:02:50.816
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.640
In a Socialist economy,
one is expected

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.390
to work for the common interest,
not one's own interest.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:00.730
But that's not
easy to accomplish.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.550
For self-interest is,
in the living universe,

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:07.450
a force as all pervasive
and ineluctable as gravity

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.670
is in the physical one.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.170
By having the
means of production

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.900
in the hands of individuals
and corporations,

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.840
capitalism both
disperses power and takes

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.970
advantage of self-interest.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:22.630
In other words, capitalism
swims downstream.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:26.800
For Socialism to work, it has
to somehow negate self-interest.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.260
It has to swim upstream.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.970
To use the analogy of gravity
again, we can negate gravity.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:36.460
That's how we got to
the moon 50 years ago

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.650
and how I got to Hillsdale
College this morning.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:39.502
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.240
But it takes energy to do so.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.900
This is why Socialist economies,
even on a very small scale,

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:51.160
have only worked for any length
of time by the use of coercion.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.520
That's why the phrase
"democratic Socialism,"

00:03:54.520 --> 00:04:00.460
a phrase much in vogue these
days, is largely an oxymoron.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.410
Now, we usually think
of the United States

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:04.930
as the most capitalist
of all major countries,

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.760
and it mostly is.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.220
While it may have been pure
coincidence that the United

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.370
States was born the very same
year that Adam Smith published

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.420
The Wealth of Nations, it
was a most fortuitous one.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.480
Alexander Hamilton, who richly
deserves the title Founding

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:23.650
Father of the American
Economy, was very familiar

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.930
with Smith's seminal
book, as were

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.670
most of the Founding Fathers.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:32.020
Hamilton took its arguments
very much to heart.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.030
When Socialism was
rapidly evolving

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:37.840
into a coherent philosophy
3/4 of a century later,

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.710
Karl Marx wrote in The 18th
Brumaire of Louis Bonaparte,

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:45.310
published in 1852, after
Napoleon III had seized power

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:49.780
in a coup d'etat, that, quote,
"Men make their own history,

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.360
but they do not make
it as they please.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:54.220
They do not make it
under circumstances

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.190
chosen by themselves,
but under circumstances

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:01.640
directly encountered and
transmitted from the past."

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.190
But this country, unlike
the European countries

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:06.590
Marx was familiar
with, had, in fact,

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:11.330
precious little past to
be transmitted in 1776.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.190
We were not a
fully-formed society

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.710
with deeply entrenched
economic interests

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.590
and a well-developed
class system.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:21.530
In effect, we were something
like a national tabula

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.070
rasa in the 1780s and '90s.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:28.310
So more than almost any
other country in history,

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:31.340
the United States could
make itself as it pleased,

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:35.370
in ways that deeply suited
the American character.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:37.250
It is worth noting
that all but one

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.530
of the 10 amendments
to the Constitution,

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:42.920
known as the Bill of
Rights, protect individuals

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.110
from excessive government power.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.780
It is this character,
this passion

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.380
to do as we damn well please
under the rule of law,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:56.430
that has made this country such
stony ground for Socialism.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:59.230
Now, the American
colonies benefited greatly

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.500
from the mother
country's benign neglect.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.480
Unlike France and
Spain, England didn't

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.060
care who went to its
colonies and, indeed,

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.410
used the colonies as a dumping
ground for convicted felons.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:14.440
It was Britain's inability to
continue transporting felons

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:18.100
to America after the American
Revolution that led directly

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:21.880
to the settlement of
Australia in 1788.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:24.475
Perhaps they should
celebrate July 4th, too.

00:06:24.475 --> 00:06:25.465
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.050
That meant that the
population of early America

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.090
was largely made
up of people who

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.180
marched to the beat
of a distant drummer.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.370
Unlike their contemporaries
in the poor economy

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:38.620
of early 17th
century Britain, they

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:41.560
chose to take an
enormous chance.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:45.310
They said goodbye to all that
they had known and came to,

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:49.630
what one Puritan called a
"howling wilderness," in hopes

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.670
of making better
and more prosperous

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:55.240
lives for themselves
and their posterity.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:58.600
As to how big a chance they
were taking, consider this.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.640
In early Virginia,
the death rate

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.230
for immigrants in their
first year was about 25%.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.960
After that, they were
said to be seasoned,

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.587
and their life expectancy
increased considerably.

00:07:11.587 --> 00:07:13.420
And these risk-taking
immigrants, of course,

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:16.330
have been coming ever since.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:18.310
Thus, the propensity
for taking risk

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:21.790
was bred into the very
DNA of this country.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:24.400
And the twin engines
of capitalist success

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:27.910
or a willingness to take
risk and competition,

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.480
neither of which are found
in Socialist economies, which

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:35.230
is why they are both
un-innovative and poor.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:38.740
Even the DNA of those
felonious transportees,

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.780
a total of about 100,000
during the Colonial era,

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:47.617
added a certain je ne sais quoi
to the American genetic mix.

00:07:47.617 --> 00:07:49.450
That is not to say that
this country has had

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:51.400
no experience with Socialism.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.800
Indeed, the early English
settlements in this country

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.350
were, at first,
Socialist enterprises,

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.150
even though the
very word Socialism

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:01.760
wouldn't be coined until 1833.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.640
The colony of Virginia
was not established

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:05.470
by the English government.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.440
Instead, it was founded by
a profit-seeking corporation

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:11.650
chartered by King
James I in 1606,

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:14.450
the Virginia Company of London.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:18.430
The stock was mostly owned by
very wealthy London merchants.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:21.010
These were very
savvy businessmen.

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:24.280
But the company they founded
was in a wholly new business,

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.830
American plantations.

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:29.560
And the steep learning
curve to profitability

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:32.620
that any new type of
business necessarily entails

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:36.700
would not be successfully
climbed in this case.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:40.360
The Virginia Company invested
all told about 50,000 pounds

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.390
sterling in the
enterprise, a colossal sum

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:46.450
in the early 17th century,
when the annual Crown

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:48.430
revenues from the
tariff amounted

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:51.610
to only about 75,000 pounds.

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:55.220
Regardless, the company
had collapsed by 1624,

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:58.780
and Virginia became
a Crown colony.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.870
Indeed, the Virginia Company
made just about every mistake

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:03.880
that it could have made.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:06.490
Jamestown was sited
badly, surrounded

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:08.830
by mosquito-breeding
swamps, and where

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:10.930
the water was often brackish.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:13.990
This led to salt poisoning
during the hot humid Virginia

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:15.610
summers.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:17.950
But the Virginia
Company's biggest mistake

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:22.130
was to keep title to the
land, the means of production.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:23.920
The settlers were,
in effect, to work it

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:27.410
as peasants for the
company's profit.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:30.280
But the company also told
them that any gold they found

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:33.510
was theirs to keep or sell.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.450
The settlers that the
Company had recruited,

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.840
all men in the first year
or so, another mistake,

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.530
were mostly what were then
called "sturdy beggars,"

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.810
i.e., healthy men
who had no employment

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:48.480
and had gravitated to the
burgeoning slums of London

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.520
and Bristol along
with the younger

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.020
sons of gentry families.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:55.140
The first group knew
little about farming,

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.930
and the latter was
disinclined to do hard work.

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:01.867
Pursuing their self-interest,
as people always do,

00:10:01.867 --> 00:10:03.700
they preferred to search
for gold from which

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:05.860
they could profit directly.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.480
Of course, there
is no gold to be

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:12.220
found in the broad alluvial
plains of tidewater Virginia,

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.380
although they found
plenty of a form of mica

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:16.330
that looked a bit like gold.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:20.390
They shipped it to England where
it was found to be worthless.

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:22.450
The result was starvation.

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:28.090
Of the 104 settlers who had
arrived in May, 1607, only 38

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:31.180
were still alive
nine months later.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:35.530
In December, 1609, the
colony had 220 settlers.

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:39.760
By spring, 1610, only
60 were still alive.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:45.460
One man, crazed by hunger,
had killed and eaten his wife.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.110
He was burned at the stake.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.710
The company tried other
means to generate income.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:53.150
They established a glass
factory, for instance,

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:56.650
to take advantage of Virginia's
abundant sand and wood.

00:10:56.650 --> 00:11:00.700
But the glass could not be
profitably exported to England.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:04.570
Two developments
saved the colony.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:08.050
One was the discovery
of a cash crop, tobacco.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:10.690
After the Indians had told
the colonists the exacting

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:14.680
art of growing and curing it,
tobacco became very profitable

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.210
indeed.

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:19.240
The second was the abandonment
of the company's policy

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:22.330
of holding title to the
land and expecting colonists

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:24.940
to work it as sharecroppers.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.940
Indeed, the company began to
use early America's greatest

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:33.770
resource, its infinity of
land, to entice settlers.

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:35.770
They started to give every
settler, who had paid

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:39.070
his own way, 50 acres
of land, along with 50

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:43.350
acres for each relative and
servant brought to the colony.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:48.040
Thus, a family of five
would get 250 acres of land.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:51.820
That much good agricultural
land in land-poor England

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:54.910
would have given that
family gentry status.

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:59.120
It was a powerful and
thoroughly capitalist incentive.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.390
Those who would come
as indentured servants

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:05.580
were also given land once they
had served their indentures.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:09.150
Food production rose sharply,
and tobacco production

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:13.350
soared, from 20,000
pounds in 1618 to 1

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:17.440
and 1/2 million pounds
only 11 years later.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:19.740
The American experiment
was off and running,

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:24.270
and Virginia's early
Socialist economy forgotten.

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.790
Early New England,
which was also

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.460
settled by profit-seeking
joint stock companies,

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.800
saw the land held
in common, as well,

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.560
and thus saw poor results.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:36.540
In the Plymouth Colony,
despite the story

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:41.890
of the first Thanksgiving, the
harvest of 1621 had been poor.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:45.180
In early 1622, Governor
William Bradford

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:47.560
gave each family a plot of land.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:51.000
But he required that, quote,
"All profits and benefits that

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:55.500
are not got by trade, working,
fishing, or any other means,

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.590
were to be placed in a common
storehouse, so that," quote,

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:01.620
"all such persons as
are of this colony

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:05.040
are to have their meat, drink,
apparel, and all provisions out

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.965
of the common stock."

00:13:06.965 --> 00:13:08.340
In other words,
the settlers were

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:11.340
expected to work the fields,
to go out fishing and hunting,

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.890
but the results were to be
doled out to each family,

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.290
according to its
size, not according

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.820
to its contribution of labor.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:22.330
The result was a very
poor harvest in 1622

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:26.950
and severe hunger in the
winter of 1622 through '23.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.950
But Bradford, happily,
was no ideologue.

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:31.930
He could learn from experience.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:35.830
He wrote in his book, A
Plymouth Plantation, quote,

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:38.740
"The failure of that
experiment of communal service,

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:40.810
the taking away of
private property,

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.610
and the possession
of it in community

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:46.060
by a commonwealth was found
to breed much confusion

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:49.630
and discontent and retard
much employment, which

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:52.520
would have been to
the general benefit,

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:55.090
for the young men who were
most able and fit for service

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:57.190
objected to being forced
to spend their time

00:13:57.190 --> 00:14:01.060
and strength in working for
other men's wives and children

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:03.280
without any recompense."

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:05.740
The strong man or
the resourceful man

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:08.500
had no more share of
food, clothes, et cetera,

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:11.740
than the weak man, who was not
able to do a quarter of what

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:13.570
the other could.

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:15.700
This was thought injustice.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.660
The aged and graver men, who
were ranked and equalized

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:22.000
in labor, food,
clothing, et cetera,

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:23.650
with the humbler
and younger ones,

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:28.180
thought it was some indignity
and disrespect to them.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:30.730
Bradford decided that each
family could grow and keep

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.590
the food they raised.

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:36.550
The change in attitude among
the settlers was stunning.

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:40.200
The women in 1622 had stayed
home to tend the children.

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:43.420
But in 1623, as Bradford
explained, quote,

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:45.520
"The women now went
willingly into the field

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:48.730
and took their little ones
with them to set corn."

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:50.740
Food production
rose dramatically.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:52.240
Within a couple of
years, the colony

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:56.600
was able to export a
load of grain to England.

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:58.940
As the historian
Nathaniel Philbrick

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:02.120
explained in his recent history
of Plymouth Colony, quote,

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:05.690
"The pilgrims had stumbled
on the power of capitalism."

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:07.160
Although the fortunes
of the colony

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:09.900
still teetered precariously
in the years ahead,

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.580
the inhabitants
never again starved.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:15.080
Of course, New
England early turned

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.740
to trading and shipbuilding,
given the lack of a cash crop

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:22.430
in New England's stony
soil and long winters.

00:15:22.430 --> 00:15:25.030
And those early merchants
knew all about capitalism,

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:27.530
even though the word wouldn't
be invented for another couple

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:29.220
of hundred years.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:32.030
Indeed, many of them wrote
at the tops of their ledgers,

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:38.810
"In the name of God and
profit," for these pious Puritan

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:41.690
merchants saw a personal
economic success

00:15:41.690 --> 00:15:44.320
as a sign of God's grace.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:47.297
It'd be nice if Elizabeth
Warren had the same attitude.

00:15:47.297 --> 00:15:48.728
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:50.159 --> 00:15:51.590
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:58.380
Now, among the early
settlers of Colonial America

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:01.230
were many groups seeking
religious freedom.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:03.660
And the United States
remains the most religious

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:05.550
of Western nations.

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:08.400
This yearning for moral
and spiritual perfection,

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.980
so much a part of the
American character,

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:15.000
has led to a plethora of new
American-born religious sects,

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.300
such as the Church of Jesus
Christ of Latter-days, Seventh

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:21.120
Day Adventists, the Shakers,
Jehovah's Witnesses,

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:23.370
and Christian Science,
to name just a few

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:26.880
of the more successful
and long-lived.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:29.070
But it also led to
many social experiments

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:31.590
in a search for a
new way of living,

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.260
where property was
to be held in common.

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:35.910
The most famous
of these projects

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.750
was New Harmony, Indiana.

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:42.400
It was founded in 1825 by a
wealthy British industrialist

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:45.190
named Robert Owen, who
was one of the founders

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:49.420
of the movement that came to
be called Utopian Socialism.

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:51.610
It got that name, by the
way, because the founders

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:54.610
of the movement believed that
Socialism could be achieved

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.925
without class struggle
but rather by example,

00:16:57.925 --> 00:17:00.130
to demonstrate that
society could work

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:01.970
in a different and better way.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:05.079
And as with Ralph Waldo
Emerson's "better mousetrap,"

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:08.020
the world would beat
a path to your door.

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:12.460
Marx and Engels dismissed this
as utopian, and the term stuck.

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:16.210
Marx and Engels, for
once, were right.

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:17.950
Owen put his money
where his mouth was.

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:22.060
In 1825, he bought an
entire town, 20,000 acres,

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:24.369
in the southwest
corner of Indiana,

00:17:24.369 --> 00:17:26.020
plus the buildings
and infrastructure

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:29.860
from a German religious sect
that had decided to relocate.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:33.790
He paid $150,000, a
very substantial fortune

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:36.040
in the 1820s.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:38.890
People were expected to provide
their own household goods,

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:40.690
to invest their
capital in enterprises

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:43.120
that would promote
community independence

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:44.895
and social equality.

00:17:44.895 --> 00:17:46.270
They would work
for the community

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:48.880
and be paid in credits
at the town's store,

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.212
or they could buy credits there.

00:17:51.212 --> 00:17:53.710
There were troubles
from the beginning.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.020
There was a shortage
of skilled craftsmen,

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:58.180
and the community could
not produce enough goods

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:00.280
to pay its way.

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:02.170
Robert Dale Owen,
son of the founder,

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:05.260
described the people who
settled in New Harmony, quote,

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:08.500
as "a heterogeneous
collection of radicals,

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:11.200
enthusiastic devotees
to principle,

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:14.980
honest latitudinarians,
and lazy theorists,

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:17.590
with a sprinkling of
unprincipled sharpers thrown

00:18:17.590 --> 00:18:19.120
in."

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:22.030
As to the town's prospects,
he wrote that, quote,

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:24.490
"a plan which
remunerates all alike

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:27.160
will, in the present
condition of society,

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:30.640
ultimately eliminate from
a cooperative association

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:34.480
the skilled, efficient, and
industrious members, leaving

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:38.170
an ineffective and sluggish
residue, in whose hands

00:18:38.170 --> 00:18:42.400
the experiment will fail, both
socially and pecuniarily."

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:46.840
In other words, self-interest
dooms such enterprises.

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:50.320
The New Harmony experiment had
collapsed within two years,

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.570
and private property
and individual pursuit

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:54.770
came to the town.

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:57.410
Now, there have been dozens, if
not hundreds, of other attempts

00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:59.480
to establish
Socialist communities,

00:18:59.480 --> 00:19:01.790
but most have failed,
although there

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:04.390
is one in Virginia on 400
acres of what was once

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:07.340
a tobacco farm that has
been in operation for more

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:10.450
than 50 years.

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:13.630
After the Civil War, as
the country industrialized

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:17.110
at a furious pace,
the wealth inequality

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:19.810
increased at an
equally furious pace.

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:22.990
Enormous fortunes epitomized
by the 400 New York

00:19:22.990 --> 00:19:26.560
families invited to
Mrs. Astor's annual ball

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:29.260
were piled up, while
industrial workers did not

00:19:29.260 --> 00:19:31.960
get anything like a fair
share of the profits they

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:34.240
helped create.

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:37.840
The reason, of course, was that
each big industrial corporation

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.330
spoke with one voice,
while there were often

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:45.256
tens of thousands of workers,
each spoke for himself.

00:19:45.256 --> 00:19:48.390
Now industrial capitalism
was something very new

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:51.540
under the sun in the
late 19th century.

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:55.050
And the rules needed to make
it function fairly and well

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:58.330
could only be developed
from experience.

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:01.550
The stock market reforms
of the post Civil War era,

00:20:01.550 --> 00:20:03.250
the invention of
independent accountants

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:05.530
and generally-accepted
accounting principles

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:09.520
in the 1890s, the Sherman and
Clayton Anti-trust legislation

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:13.630
of 1890 and 1914, the creation
of the Interstate Commerce

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:17.950
Commission in 1887, and
the Wagner Act of 1939

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:19.540
were what resulted.

00:20:19.540 --> 00:20:23.170
It was a process that
would take about 50 years.

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:25.890
And, of course, the reforms
have needed their own reforms

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:27.458
over the years.

00:20:27.458 --> 00:20:29.500
The Interstate Commerce
Commission, for instance,

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:32.430
was meant as a check on the
unbridled economic power

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:33.930
of railroads.

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:36.300
But it quickly
evolved into a cartel,

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:38.160
protecting the
railroads' profits

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:42.120
and greatly inhibiting
innovation in that industry.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:45.790
When its rate-setting powers
were abolished in the 1970s,

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:48.720
so that railroads and trucking
firms could once again compete

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:51.450
by means of price, the
transportation sector

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:56.520
of the GDP dropped
from 15% to 10%.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:59.610
As transportation is what
economists call a transaction

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:01.950
cost, a necessary
cost that does not

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:04.540
increase the value
of the product,

00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:06.840
the prices of
millions of products

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:09.960
dropped as competition
forced manufacturers

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:15.150
to share the lower costs of
transportation with consumers.

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:17.150
It should be noted that
we are, today, beginning

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:20.060
to learn what rules are
needed to properly regulate

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:22.130
the new digital
economy we now live

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:27.500
in that has caused another
surge in income inequality.

00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:29.960
Meanwhile, there
were increased calls

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.000
for Socialism in
American politics

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.120
as a way to abate this
increasing inequality,

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:37.370
just as there are today.

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:39.200
Among the leaders
of this movement

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:44.280
was Eugene V. Debs, born in
1855 in Terre Haute, Indiana.

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:45.760
He came from a
middle class family.

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:48.380
His father owned a
small textile business.

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:51.650
But he dropped out of school at
the age of 14 and went to work

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:55.580
for a local railroad
earning $0.50 a day.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:57.770
He quickly identified
with the working class

00:21:57.770 --> 00:22:01.610
and its economic problems
in the new Industrial Age.

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:04.770
Debs was a founder of
an early labor union,

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:06.380
the Industrial
Workers of the World--

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:08.390
remembered as the Wobblies--

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:11.120
as well as the
American Railway Union.

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:13.850
The Pullman Strike
of 1894, led by Debs

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:15.980
after an initial
hesitation, was one

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:19.940
of a series of often violent
strikes at this time.

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:22.070
The Pullman Strike resulted
in about $80 million

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:25.430
worth of damage and 13 deaths.

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:27.500
Charged with contempt
of court for violating

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:29.870
a federal injunction
against the strike,

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:32.000
he served six months in jail.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:33.830
During that time,
Debs read widely

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:36.980
about international Socialism
and emerged from jail

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:39.768
a firm believer in it.

00:22:39.768 --> 00:22:43.090
Originally a Democrat-- he had
served two terms in the Indiana

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:45.910
House of Representatives
as a Democrat--

00:22:45.910 --> 00:22:48.760
Debs was a founding member of
several Socialist political

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:50.410
parties.

00:22:50.410 --> 00:22:54.220
Socialism was never able to
form a single coherent political

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:56.980
party in this country,
as Socialist leaders--

00:22:56.980 --> 00:22:59.620
almost entirely
intellectuals-- endlessly

00:22:59.620 --> 00:23:02.500
split over ideological minutia.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:05.200
There were social Democrats,
anarchists, Communists,

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:08.200
syndicalists, and
many, many more.

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:12.020
Debs joined the Socialist
Party of America in 1901,

00:23:12.020 --> 00:23:15.250
a party formed that year from a
merger of the Social Democratic

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.950
Party and the
Socialist Labor Party.

00:23:17.950 --> 00:23:21.100
He ran for president on
its ticket five times.

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:25.480
In 1912, he received
905,000 votes, fully

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:28.480
6% of the total vote.

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:31.810
In 1920, he won
3.4% of the vote,

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:34.700
although he was in jail
for sedition at the time.

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:35.620
[CHUCKLING]

00:23:35.620 --> 00:23:37.360
The Socialist
Party was adamantly

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:40.030
opposed to American
participation in World War I,

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:43.660
and Debs had spoken out in
favor of resisting the draft.

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:46.360
Sentenced to 10 years,
President Warren Harding

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:50.900
commuted the sentence to time
served in December of 1921,

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:54.370
after Debs had served
nearly three years in jail.

00:23:54.370 --> 00:23:56.860
After the collapse of the
Czarist regime in Russia

00:23:56.860 --> 00:23:59.950
and the subsequent
takeover by the Bolsheviks,

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:03.190
along with this country's
entry into World War I,

00:24:03.190 --> 00:24:06.070
the United States experienced
the first of the two

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:09.010
20th century Red Scares.

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:10.990
There were reasons
for these scares.

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:13.570
There were numerous attempts--
some successful-- to bomb

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:15.380
people and property.

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:17.950
The Washington house of Woodrow
Wilson's attorney general,

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:19.960
Mitchell Palmer,
was badly damaged

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:24.100
by a bomb in June, 1919,
although Palmer himself was not

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:25.720
seriously hurt.

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:29.710
And in September, 1920, a
whole wagon-load of dynamite

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:32.710
blew up in front of the
Morgan Bank on Wall Street,

00:24:32.710 --> 00:24:37.540
killing 38 and injuring
hundreds, 143 of them severely.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:41.080
Windows were blown
out for blocks around.

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:44.500
The facade of the Morgan Bank
was scarred by the explosion,

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:48.010
and the bank decided not to
repair the damage, leaving it

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:51.170
as a sort of stigmata
of capitalism.

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:53.650
It's still there, by the way,
a minor Wall Street tourist

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:55.840
attraction.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:57.630
But the fear generated
by the Red Scare

00:24:57.630 --> 00:25:00.450
was far greater than
the actual threat.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.430
Hundreds of aliens
were deported,

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:05.820
including such well-known ones
as Emma Goldman and Alexander

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:08.940
Berkman, who had tried to
assassinate the industrialist

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:10.890
Henry Clay Frick.

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:13.770
By one of those wonderful
coincidences that history

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:16.650
is so full of, the
day he was deported

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:20.460
was the day that Henry Clay
Frick died of natural causes.

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:23.220
And Berkman said, "At least he
left the country before I did."

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:25.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:27.440
While the Socialist
Party never came

00:25:27.440 --> 00:25:30.170
anywhere close to winning
a presidential race,

00:25:30.170 --> 00:25:33.950
it had electoral success at the
local and congressional levels.

00:25:33.950 --> 00:25:37.040
After the election of 1920 put
five Socialists in the New York

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:40.100
State Assembly, they
were summarily suspended

00:25:40.100 --> 00:25:41.720
and then expelled.

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:45.140
They all ran in special
elections and were re-elected,

00:25:45.140 --> 00:25:48.740
but, again, denied their seats.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:50.510
The Communist Party
of the United States

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:54.260
was founded in 1919 after
the Russian Revolution,

00:25:54.260 --> 00:25:58.040
as a split-off from Debs'
Socialist Party of America.

00:25:58.040 --> 00:26:01.640
Unlike Debs' Socialists, it
supported the dictatorship

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:04.340
of the proletariat,
which is to say,

00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:07.190
the dictatorship of
self-appointed fiduciaries

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:09.470
for the proletariat.

00:26:09.470 --> 00:26:12.410
It was deeply involved in
the American Labor Movement

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:14.560
and, through a variety
of front organizations,

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:17.240
influenced many
prominent people.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:18.950
During the Great
Depression, membership

00:26:18.950 --> 00:26:23.570
grew to 55,000, at least
according to the CPUSA.

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:25.610
But it was, from
the first, largely

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:30.530
funded by and took orders
from the Soviet Union.

00:26:30.530 --> 00:26:34.100
Immediately after World War II
broke out in September, 1939,

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:36.840
for instance, the Communist
Party's newspaper,

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:40.040
The Daily Worker, began a
long and frequent series

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:42.650
of editorials, demanding
that this country stay out

00:26:42.650 --> 00:26:44.350
of the war.

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:48.160
But on June 23, 1941,
the paper, without even

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:50.830
noting its about-face,
began demanding

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:54.490
the immediate entrance of the
United States into the war.

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:57.910
What had caused it to
change its editorial minds?

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:03.370
Nazi Germany invaded the
Soviet Union on June 22, 1941.

00:27:03.370 --> 00:27:06.040
This convinced many that the
Communist Party of the United

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:09.190
States was not a genuine
American political party

00:27:09.190 --> 00:27:12.170
but a foreign agent.

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:14.840
Non-Communist Socialism's
political popularity

00:27:14.840 --> 00:27:17.360
waned in the 1920s
in this country,

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:19.130
while increasing in Europe.

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:23.900
Both Britain and France elected
Socialist governments in 1924.

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:26.780
But Norman Thomas received
only 2% of the vote

00:27:26.780 --> 00:27:29.960
in the election of 1932,
despite the country

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:32.150
being in the depths of
the Great Depression,

00:27:32.150 --> 00:27:35.360
while there was widespread
fear that capitalism and even

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:38.600
democracy would not survive.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:43.130
New Deal programs and especially
the GI Bill of Rights in 1944

00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:47.040
proved the death knell
of Socialism in America.

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:50.790
By subsidizing both college
educations and home ownership,

00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:54.120
the GI Bill lifted millions
into the middle class

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:56.880
with financial assets,
such as real estate.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:28:01.570
In other words, the
proletariat became capitalists.

00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:03.930
But at the same time,
so a new Red Scare--

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:06.390
thanks to the onset
of the Cold War--

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:09.270
people like Senator Joseph
McCarthy and the House

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:12.030
Un-American Activities
Committee hounded people

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.130
before television
cameras, but found

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:16.440
relatively few
actual Communists,

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:19.410
while ruining many
lives and careers.

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:23.430
Finally, in 1954, Senator
McCarthy overplayed his hand,

00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:25.170
was censured by the Senate.

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:29.590
And the second Red Scare
began to fade away.

00:28:29.590 --> 00:28:32.070
While Socialism disappeared
as a political force

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:34.530
after World War
II, its last gasp

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:36.330
was probably the
presidential campaign

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.910
of Henry Wallace in 1948.

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:41.730
Conservatives have worried
about at what point

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:47.560
the social safety net ends and
latter-day Socialism begins.

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:49.990
The Democratic Party has
moved markedly leftward

00:28:49.990 --> 00:28:51.460
in the last few years.

00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:53.290
And, today, we are,
once again, hearing

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:56.590
calls from the far left
for Socialist policies,

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:59.980
such as Medicare for All
and egalitarian policies,

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:03.790
such as a punitive wealth tax on
those who have been excessively

00:29:03.790 --> 00:29:05.940
successful.

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:08.460
Britain tried this tactic
in the Labout government

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:11.850
of Clement Attlee after World
War II, when the top income tax

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:13.690
rate in Britain was 104%.

00:29:13.690 --> 00:29:15.750
[CHUCKLING]

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:19.920
In other words, you were fined
for creating too much wealth.

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:21.480
That goes a long
way to explaining

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:26.290
why Britain's economic recovery
after the war was so sluggish.

00:29:26.290 --> 00:29:28.630
Senator Bernie Sanders,
himself a millionaire

00:29:28.630 --> 00:29:31.630
who owns three houses,
says that no one should

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:35.380
be worth a billion dollars
and would impose an 8% tax

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:38.420
on the very, very rich,
most of whom, by the way,

00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:39.520
made their own fortune.

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:44.740
70% of the Forbes 400 are
self-made men and women.

00:29:44.740 --> 00:29:46.720
And no one, of
course, is any poorer

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:48.580
because of the
digital mega fortunes

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:52.110
that have been created
in the last 40 years.

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:55.140
Enriching the rich by
immiserating the poor is

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:57.270
very poor public policy.

00:29:57.270 --> 00:30:00.000
Just ask Louis XVI.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.520
Becoming super rich,
as Jeff Bezos has,

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:05.520
by giving everybody a better
and cheaper way to shop

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:08.210
made all of us richer.

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:10.130
Ideologues, like
Bernie Sanders--

00:30:10.130 --> 00:30:14.090
he had his honeymoon in the
Soviet Union, please remember--

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:17.790
Stephen Sondheim once
wrote, "I've been to Moscow.

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:21.554
It's very gay, well,
anyway, on the 1st of May."

00:30:21.554 --> 00:30:22.946
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:29.180
These ideologues are often
invincibly ignorant of the most

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:31.250
basic economics.

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:33.170
Not only would a
wealth tax be extremely

00:30:33.170 --> 00:30:35.670
hard and expensive
to administer,

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:37.700
it would have
devastating effects

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:42.620
on the entire American economy
and the American population.

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:45.830
If, say, Jeff Bezos had
to pay 8% of his net worth

00:30:45.830 --> 00:30:48.140
in taxes every
year, he would have

00:30:48.140 --> 00:30:52.580
to sell 8% of his vast
holdings of Amazon stock.

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.920
That, of course, would cause
the price of Amazon stock

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:57.230
to drastically decline.

00:30:57.230 --> 00:31:00.350
Indeed, just the market's
anticipation of the sale

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:04.250
would cause it to decline,
costing Bezos far more than 8%

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:06.080
of his net worth.

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:09.380
And Amazon stock is held
not just by Jeff Bezos,

00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:12.560
but directly or indirectly
by millions of middle-class

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:17.300
Americans in their 401(k)
accounts, IRAs, pension funds,

00:31:17.300 --> 00:31:18.920
and annuity accounts.

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:21.560
About 2/3 of
corporate stock today

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:26.280
is held not by plutocrats
but by ordinary Americans.

00:31:26.280 --> 00:31:28.990
Multiply Jeff Bezos'
woes under a wealth

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:33.220
tax by those of the other 620
billionaires in the country

00:31:33.220 --> 00:31:36.370
today, and you would have
a stock market crash that

00:31:36.370 --> 00:31:39.710
would be worse than 1929.

00:31:39.710 --> 00:31:43.450
So a wealth tax, while
sticking it to the Forbes 400,

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:45.580
would also stick to
just about everyone

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:49.180
in this room, not to mention
Hillsdale College's endowment

00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:51.160
fund.

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:54.220
I expect that these policies
will get exactly nowhere.

00:31:54.220 --> 00:31:56.650
The wealth tax is, after
all, almost certainly

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:59.830
unconstitutional, as
it is a direct tax that

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:01.960
could not be apportioned
among the states

00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:04.190
according to population.

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:07.480
You could also make a good case
that it is a bill of attainder.

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:09.880
After all, Forbes
magazine will supply you

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:14.230
with a list of the 621
billionaires in this country.

00:32:14.230 --> 00:32:16.030
The overwhelming
majority of Americans

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:18.160
celebrate capitalist
success, not

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:21.460
decry it like Bernie Sanders
and Elizabeth Warren,

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:23.920
and do not want to kill
the capitalist goose that

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:28.680
has laid so very many golden
eggs for so many people.

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:32.160
They understand instinctively,
thanks to those risk-taking

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:36.000
entrepreneurial genes they
inherited, that Socialism--

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:38.580
to borrow a phrase
from George Orwell--

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:41.820
is an "idea so stupid
that only an intellectual

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:42.570
could believe it."

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:45.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:49.760
The United States is still very
stony ground for Socialism.

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:51.157
Thank you very much.

00:32:51.157 --> 00:32:52.648
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:03.100
SPEAKER: Thank you, Mr. Gordon.

00:33:03.100 --> 00:33:05.643
We now have time for some
Q&amp;A. If you have a question,

00:33:05.643 --> 00:33:07.810
please make your way to the
microphone in the aisle.

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:17.320
AUDIENCE: Yes, sir.

00:33:17.320 --> 00:33:19.860
The one thing you brought
up, or at least touched

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:25.490
on tangentially, was
income inequality.

00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:29.810
How do you feel about the
concept of the cooperative?

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:34.460
Does that give capitalism
a bit of a safety valve?

00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:36.230
I know it works.

00:33:36.230 --> 00:33:41.480
I mean, in a small sense,
it's part of our economy,

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:43.288
cooperative structures.

00:33:43.288 --> 00:33:45.080
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
You know, a corporation

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:46.640
is a cooperative structure.

00:33:46.640 --> 00:33:48.740
People come together
under a defined set

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:51.800
of rules in hopes of making more
money collectively than they

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:54.340
could make individually.

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:56.970
I mean, so there's nothing
wrong with cooperative ventures.

00:33:56.970 --> 00:33:59.490
And if the people who are in
that want to do it that way,

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:00.380
that's fine.

00:34:00.380 --> 00:34:02.840
But the income
inequality, every time

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:05.720
you have serious
new technology, you

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:08.540
have a sudden inflorescene
of new fortunes

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:11.195
that are larger than
the old ones were.

00:34:11.195 --> 00:34:14.929
I mean, in 1827, a young
British writer coined

00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:18.110
the word "millionaire"
in order to describe,

00:34:18.110 --> 00:34:22.250
not the old aristocracy whose
wealth was based on land,

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:24.949
but the new industrialists,
who were much richer,

00:34:24.949 --> 00:34:26.549
usually in terms of cash.

00:34:26.549 --> 00:34:29.989
The aristocrats are often
cash poor or land poor,

00:34:29.989 --> 00:34:32.929
as they call it.

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:34.880
And by the way,
that young writer

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:36.260
went on to another profession.

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:37.760
His name was Benjamin Disraeli.

00:34:37.760 --> 00:34:38.718
[CHUCKLING]

00:34:40.159 --> 00:34:41.960
It happened again in
the late 19th century

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:44.429
with railroads and
automobiles and what have you.

00:34:44.429 --> 00:34:48.260
And now it is happening with
digital technology, fortunes

00:34:48.260 --> 00:34:52.520
that were simply inconceivable
even 40 years ago.

00:34:52.520 --> 00:34:55.850
I mean, 1982 was the first
year of the Forbes 400,

00:34:55.850 --> 00:34:58.400
and you needed $82
million to make the list.

00:34:58.400 --> 00:34:59.900
And the richest
guy was a guy named

00:34:59.900 --> 00:35:03.470
Ludwig, who nobody remembers
anymore, who was worth,

00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:06.230
I think, it was $2.1 billion.

00:35:06.230 --> 00:35:11.060
Today, you need $2.1 billion
to get onto the Forbes list.

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:17.310
There are about 200
American billionaires not

00:35:17.310 --> 00:35:20.790
rich enough to make that list.

00:35:20.790 --> 00:35:24.180
Now, nobody's gotten poorer
because of these new fortunes.

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:26.058
This is wealth that was created.

00:35:26.058 --> 00:35:26.725
It wasn't taken.

00:35:26.725 --> 00:35:30.260
I mean, Elizabeth
Warren thinks capitalism

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:34.170
is a zero-sum game like
poker, where for every winner,

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:35.730
you have to have a loser.

00:35:35.730 --> 00:35:37.890
We are all winners.

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:38.970
We are all winners.

00:35:38.970 --> 00:35:41.610
Anybody who's ever bought
something from Amazon

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:46.230
or used Twitter or, you know,
found out where the hell

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:53.070
you were through the GPS on your
cell phone, we are all winners.

00:35:53.070 --> 00:35:56.467
There's no losers, which I
wish people who just get that

00:35:56.467 --> 00:35:57.300
through their heads.

00:35:57.300 --> 00:36:00.420
I think they actually understand
it, but they just politically--

00:36:00.420 --> 00:36:04.507
you know, politicians
lie like rugs.

00:36:04.507 --> 00:36:05.968
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:10.195 --> 00:36:12.070
AUDIENCE: Yeah, really
kind of two questions.

00:36:12.070 --> 00:36:14.980
One quick one-- when
the polls mention

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:19.480
people between the
ages of 18 and 25, 50%

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:22.210
say they would like to live
in a Socialist country.

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:25.280
Do they really understand
what Socialist means?

00:36:25.280 --> 00:36:28.690
Do they understand
that the government is

00:36:28.690 --> 00:36:30.790
the employer, is controlling?

00:36:30.790 --> 00:36:34.060
Are they just
economically illiterate?

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:38.022
And, secondly, could you
comment on capitalism in China?

00:36:38.022 --> 00:36:38.980
JOHN STEELE GORDON: OK.

00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:44.890
I think an awful lot of
those 18 to 24-year-olds

00:36:44.890 --> 00:36:48.730
went to propaganda mills
masquerading as universities.

00:36:48.730 --> 00:36:49.694
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:50.658 --> 00:36:51.622
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:56.450 --> 00:36:59.300
They just-- obviously, they
don't understand economics.

00:36:59.300 --> 00:37:01.070
They don't understand
human nature.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:03.440
I mean, that's why
Socialism can't work because

00:37:03.440 --> 00:37:05.930
of human nature, because
we are self-interested,

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:08.390
and we pursue our
self interests.

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:11.280
And you can't-- people
can't stop doing that.

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:12.780
And, you know, a
lot of people think

00:37:12.780 --> 00:37:14.540
that self-interest is greed.

00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:17.090
No, it's not.

00:37:17.090 --> 00:37:19.790
I mean, Mother Teresa
was self-interested.

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:21.770
And what made her happy
was spending her life

00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:24.410
in the slums of Calcutta.

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:28.400
What made Jeff Bezos happy
was making $160 billion.

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:29.380
[CHUCKLING]

00:37:31.340 --> 00:37:33.830
And as for capitalism
in China, China

00:37:33.830 --> 00:37:37.400
is a very weird
hybrid at this moment.

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:39.080
It is capitalist in some ways.

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:42.460
But, of course, it is a
complete tyranny in others.

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:45.500
And there's still huge state
enterprises, most of which

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:47.840
are very inefficient, as
state enterprises tend to be.

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:51.470
And they keep getting bucked
up by the Chinese government.

00:37:51.470 --> 00:37:56.390
I don't think that's a stable
system in the long run.

00:37:56.390 --> 00:37:58.430
And China, you
know, remember, you

00:37:58.430 --> 00:37:59.980
can't trust Chinese statistics.

00:37:59.980 --> 00:38:03.650
They make them up, just
like the Soviet Union did.

00:38:03.650 --> 00:38:06.328
There's a lot of unrest
that you don't hear about

00:38:06.328 --> 00:38:08.870
because if reporters report it,
they suddenly find themselves

00:38:08.870 --> 00:38:11.390
no longer in China.

00:38:11.390 --> 00:38:14.930
But only about, I think it's
65% of the Chinese population is

00:38:14.930 --> 00:38:15.920
Han Chinese.

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:20.390
The rest are all Uyghurs
out in Xinjiang, Manchus

00:38:20.390 --> 00:38:21.500
up in Manchuria.

00:38:21.500 --> 00:38:24.770
There's all kinds of
ethnic minorities in China,

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:27.200
and many of them are not happy.

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:29.880
Now, China has got firm control.

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:32.820
I mean, they're not going to
go gentle into that good night,

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:34.590
I promise you.

00:38:34.590 --> 00:38:36.120
But I don't think,
in the long term,

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:40.600
you can have this capitalist
tyranny sustained,

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:44.080
but we will see.

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:47.560
AUDIENCE: My question relates
to the last gentleman.

00:38:47.560 --> 00:38:49.540
You mentioned that
Socialism so far

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:52.240
has had a stony ground
in the United States.

00:38:52.240 --> 00:38:54.640
With the plurality
of millennials

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:57.430
embracing Socialism or
appearing to embrace Socialism,

00:38:57.430 --> 00:38:59.960
do you find that a little
bit of an alarming statistic?

00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:02.530
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
Yeah, it's very alarming.

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:05.980
But I think, once they get
out into the real world,

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:10.410
they'll probably
have to wise up.

00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:12.940
I mean, show me a
Socialist economy

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:14.560
that has been successful.

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:16.152
There aren't any.

00:39:16.152 --> 00:39:17.860
So why should we have
a Socialist economy

00:39:17.860 --> 00:39:19.693
here when we've had the
most success economy

00:39:19.693 --> 00:39:21.470
in the history of the world?

00:39:21.470 --> 00:39:24.700
So let's get rid of
that idea that works

00:39:24.700 --> 00:39:27.870
and get one that has a proven
track record of failure?

00:39:27.870 --> 00:39:29.230
It's just-- the mind boggles.

00:39:32.150 --> 00:39:33.630
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for your talk.

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:35.213
I was wondering, you
mentioned earlier

00:39:35.213 --> 00:39:39.350
that there was an experiment on
the East Coast of a Socialist,

00:39:39.350 --> 00:39:42.160
like a community that
did work, and that

00:39:42.160 --> 00:39:44.150
still survived for 50 years.

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:45.150
Why is that?

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:47.400
JOHN STEELE GORDON: Well,
it's a very small community,

00:39:47.400 --> 00:39:50.680
and people are free
to come and go.

00:39:50.680 --> 00:39:53.590
And so people, they try it
out for a couple of years,

00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:57.300
and then they move on,
and other people move in.

00:39:57.300 --> 00:40:00.830
And I think it's not a tyranny.

00:40:00.830 --> 00:40:02.130
People are free to come and go.

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:04.520
They're not forced to be there.

00:40:04.520 --> 00:40:07.480
And they can go outside it
and earn money on the outside.

00:40:07.480 --> 00:40:09.540
I mean, they're free
people, who choose

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:12.960
to live in this
very small community

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:14.600
and do things together.

00:40:14.600 --> 00:40:18.330
And I think there's 60, 70
people who live on that place.

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:23.550
And that small a group can do
very well, just like, you know,

00:40:23.550 --> 00:40:27.810
hunter-gatherer groups in the
Paleolithic did very well.

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:30.671
And potentially, it was
the same kind of system.

00:40:30.671 --> 00:40:32.114
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:37.900 --> 00:40:41.770
AUDIENCE: Can you comment on our
fiat currency and its perceived

00:40:41.770 --> 00:40:44.275
stability in our
capitalistic economy?

00:40:44.275 --> 00:40:46.150
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
Would you say that again?

00:40:46.150 --> 00:40:50.050
AUDIENCE: Can you comment on
our fiat currency's stability

00:40:50.050 --> 00:40:51.880
and how you think
that might impact

00:40:51.880 --> 00:40:53.262
our capitalistic economy?

00:40:53.262 --> 00:40:54.220
JOHN STEELE GORDON: OK.

00:40:54.220 --> 00:40:58.060
Well, every country in the
world now has fiat currencies.

00:40:58.060 --> 00:41:03.430
And what keeps them honest is
the currency-trading market,

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:07.390
which trades around
$5 trillion a day.

00:41:07.390 --> 00:41:12.220
And in the early 1980s,
the president of France,

00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:17.200
Francois Mitterrand,
tried a Socialist agenda.

00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:21.370
He nationalized the French
banks and on and on and on.

00:41:21.370 --> 00:41:25.455
The currency market tanked,
the French currency,

00:41:25.455 --> 00:41:26.830
to the point where
Mitterrand had

00:41:26.830 --> 00:41:30.280
no alternative but to back off.

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:32.980
And that's what-- we
got rid of inflation.

00:41:32.980 --> 00:41:37.080
In the 1970s, inflation
roared in this country.

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:43.370
In 1980, the prime rate was
20%, which is really awesome.

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:46.810
But then the currency
market got big enough.

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:50.200
It got globalized enough so
it can act as a single unit

00:41:50.200 --> 00:41:51.170
basically.

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:52.600
And that's what
keeps politicians

00:41:52.600 --> 00:41:55.360
from printing too
much of the stuff.

00:41:55.360 --> 00:41:59.650
And occasionally, there are
countries like Argentina, which

00:41:59.650 --> 00:42:03.751
does it over and
over and over again,

00:42:03.751 --> 00:42:06.490
and then they keep
reelecting Peronistas

00:42:06.490 --> 00:42:08.380
over and over and over again.

00:42:08.380 --> 00:42:10.360
It's really-- Argentina
is a wonderful country,

00:42:10.360 --> 00:42:13.070
but their politics
is just bizarre.

00:42:13.070 --> 00:42:15.417
It makes French
politics look good.

00:42:15.417 --> 00:42:16.391
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:18.830 --> 00:42:22.350
I think our money's under
control at the moment.

00:42:22.350 --> 00:42:24.430
And we also have a--

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:26.600
if Congress could
print money, then we'd

00:42:26.600 --> 00:42:27.560
be in serious trouble.

00:42:27.560 --> 00:42:28.185
But they can't.

00:42:28.185 --> 00:42:30.020
Only the Fed can do that.

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:32.150
That's one that
Hamilton said, you know,

00:42:32.150 --> 00:42:34.610
don't let politicians
anywhere near

00:42:34.610 --> 00:42:35.800
the power of printing money.

00:42:39.900 --> 00:42:44.410
AUDIENCE: One of the most
effective enemies of capitalism

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:46.940
are the capitalists themselves.

00:42:46.940 --> 00:42:49.280
Bill Gates is giving
away his money

00:42:49.280 --> 00:42:54.950
by the billions, trying to buy
himself a Mother Teresa halo.

00:42:54.950 --> 00:42:55.950
Why are they doing that?

00:42:55.950 --> 00:42:58.950
What is their view of
morality that they consider--

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:01.590
Mother Teresa would
help the few people out

00:43:01.590 --> 00:43:03.900
of the gutter better
than the people

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:05.100
that [INAUDIBLE] the gutter.

00:43:05.100 --> 00:43:06.892
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
Herbert Hoover famously

00:43:06.892 --> 00:43:10.440
said that the trouble with
capitalism is capitalists.

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:12.780
And he's right because
capitalists are all

00:43:12.780 --> 00:43:16.252
too happy-- or many of them
are-- to become rent-seekers

00:43:16.252 --> 00:43:17.460
if they can get away with it.

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:22.350
And in this time in this world,
they can very frequently.

00:43:22.350 --> 00:43:24.570
The government gives them
special little fiddles

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:26.162
on the side.

00:43:26.162 --> 00:43:32.380
But the tax code is corrupt
from page one to page 56,000.

00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:36.750
And half of it is literally
little quiet fiddles

00:43:36.750 --> 00:43:39.000
to people who make big
political contributions.

00:43:42.170 --> 00:43:47.115
But I'm afraid, again,
that's human nature, too.

00:43:47.115 --> 00:43:50.750
If you can get a cheap buck,
you're going to take it.

00:43:50.750 --> 00:43:54.960
And who can blame them?

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:56.930
So it just-- you know,
human, [STUTTERS],,

00:43:56.930 --> 00:44:00.470
we are not angels, as
James Madison so astutely

00:44:00.470 --> 00:44:02.018
pointed out.

00:44:02.018 --> 00:44:04.560
And that's true of capitalists,
and it's true of politicians,

00:44:04.560 --> 00:44:08.390
and it's true of
university professors, so--

00:44:08.390 --> 00:44:10.406
even the occasional speaker.

00:44:10.406 --> 00:44:11.318
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:17.770
AUDIENCE: So according to the
World Health Care Organization,

00:44:17.770 --> 00:44:20.260
the American health
care is around,

00:44:20.260 --> 00:44:23.630
I think, 36th on the list,
whereas the French health

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:25.600
care and Canadian
health care are

00:44:25.600 --> 00:44:27.520
in front of the American ones.

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:30.850
So I was wondering, why do you
think socialized health cares

00:44:30.850 --> 00:44:33.430
like theirs can be
higher up on that list

00:44:33.430 --> 00:44:37.320
than the American one,
if they should be more

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:39.427
innovative and more functional?

00:44:39.427 --> 00:44:41.260
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
There's two things here.

00:44:41.260 --> 00:44:42.360
One is statistics.

00:44:45.670 --> 00:44:49.690
In many European
countries, a baby

00:44:49.690 --> 00:44:53.170
that dies very
shortly after birth

00:44:53.170 --> 00:44:56.405
is listed as a stillbirth,
whereas in this country,

00:44:56.405 --> 00:44:58.470
if a baby is born
struggling, they

00:44:58.470 --> 00:45:00.820
move Heaven and Earth
to keep it alive.

00:45:00.820 --> 00:45:03.830
And if they lose it,
it's an infant mortality.

00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:05.680
So we have a higher
infant mortality rate

00:45:05.680 --> 00:45:08.800
than Britain and France
and other countries do.

00:45:08.800 --> 00:45:10.848
But that's a statistical thing.

00:45:10.848 --> 00:45:12.640
The other thing is the
American health care

00:45:12.640 --> 00:45:15.770
system is a complete mess.

00:45:15.770 --> 00:45:18.670
I mean, it's the best
system in the world

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:25.090
in terms of its ability
to cure the incurable.

00:45:25.090 --> 00:45:29.670
But economically,
it is terrible.

00:45:29.670 --> 00:45:32.180
I mean, there is no
price transparency

00:45:32.180 --> 00:45:33.960
in the United States.

00:45:33.960 --> 00:45:37.400
I mean, you go to a hospital and
say, I need my knee replaced.

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:39.110
How much would you charge?

00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:40.910
They won't tell you.

00:45:40.910 --> 00:45:42.885
And then you get this
bill that's this thick,

00:45:42.885 --> 00:45:49.500
and one aspirin tablet,
$2.50, and what have you.

00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:52.770
And Donald Trump is actually
trying to get them to do this.

00:45:52.770 --> 00:45:55.080
And both the insurance
companies and the hospitals

00:45:55.080 --> 00:45:58.140
are fighting tooth and
nail to prevent it.

00:45:58.140 --> 00:46:01.740
If they were forced to post
their prices everywhere,

00:46:01.740 --> 00:46:03.240
and so every
hospital had to say,

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:07.590
we'll do a knee transplant
for x, now, immediately,

00:46:07.590 --> 00:46:08.863
what would happen?

00:46:08.863 --> 00:46:09.790
AUDIENCE: Competition.

00:46:09.790 --> 00:46:11.248
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
The competition

00:46:11.248 --> 00:46:13.200
would force people to
lower their prices.

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:16.230
And we could drain
at least $2 trillion

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:18.150
a year out of
American health care,

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:21.170
simply by making them
post their prices.

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:23.100
But the American
Medical Association,

00:46:23.100 --> 00:46:24.690
the American
Hospital Association

00:46:24.690 --> 00:46:28.270
are extremely potent
lobbies in Washington.

00:46:28.270 --> 00:46:32.690
But that would go a long
way to solving health care.

00:46:32.690 --> 00:46:35.370
We have too many hospitals
in this country, very hard

00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:36.470
politically to close them.

00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:41.990
What we need is more
specialty hospitals,

00:46:41.990 --> 00:46:45.470
like Memorial Sloan Kettering
in New York, the great cancer

00:46:45.470 --> 00:46:49.070
hospital, and then
urgent care places.

00:46:49.070 --> 00:46:52.660
And then small towns need
maybe small hospitals.

00:46:52.660 --> 00:46:55.130
And if somebody is really
sick, then they ship them off

00:46:55.130 --> 00:46:57.800
to a major metropolitan area.

00:46:57.800 --> 00:47:01.160
But we have many, many,
too many hospital beds

00:47:01.160 --> 00:47:05.460
at the moment, which
is also very expensive.

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:08.100
AUDIENCE: John, thank
you for your talk.

00:47:08.100 --> 00:47:10.050
One thing I would
recommend to everybody

00:47:10.050 --> 00:47:14.010
is they read his books, starting
with Hamilton's Blessing.

00:47:14.010 --> 00:47:16.600
It is a fantastic work.

00:47:16.600 --> 00:47:21.267
As for a question, my question
has to do with inflation.

00:47:21.267 --> 00:47:23.100
How come we don't have
it, even though we've

00:47:23.100 --> 00:47:24.330
printed so much money?

00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:27.360
Is it because of our
incredible productivity?

00:47:27.360 --> 00:47:30.270
Or is it bad data, that
we're not actually seeing

00:47:30.270 --> 00:47:33.900
it being reported correctly?

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:34.770
What do you think?

00:47:34.770 --> 00:47:36.270
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
Well, one reason

00:47:36.270 --> 00:47:37.950
is the digitalization
of the economy,

00:47:37.950 --> 00:47:42.050
as enormous efficiencies
are built into that.

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:44.170
I mean, so it's--

00:47:44.170 --> 00:47:45.380
as I say, Amazon--

00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:47.300
again, I'm always
talking about Amazon,

00:47:47.300 --> 00:47:50.085
I only go there two or
three times a week--

00:47:50.085 --> 00:47:51.035
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:52.460 --> 00:47:55.130
They're able to sell
things at a much lower cost

00:47:55.130 --> 00:47:57.730
than brick-and-mortar
because they

00:47:57.730 --> 00:48:00.560
have such enormous
economies of scale.

00:48:00.560 --> 00:48:03.140
And Walmart, too, Walmart,
its economic power's

00:48:03.140 --> 00:48:06.350
so that it can force its
suppliers to really cut things

00:48:06.350 --> 00:48:07.880
to the bone.

00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:10.850
And so that's what, I think,
has kept inflation under check

00:48:10.850 --> 00:48:13.580
in the last, this revolution
that we've been undergoing

00:48:13.580 --> 00:48:16.830
for the last 30-odd years.

00:48:16.830 --> 00:48:20.030
AUDIENCE: I haven't understood
this because the same car

00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:20.530
I bought--

00:48:20.530 --> 00:48:23.020
AUDIENCE: Sorry, sir, can
you use the microphone?

00:48:23.020 --> 00:48:24.020
AUDIENCE: The same car--

00:48:24.020 --> 00:48:25.437
AUDIENCE: Use the
microphone, sir.

00:48:25.437 --> 00:48:27.050
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
Yeah, use the mic.

00:48:27.050 --> 00:48:30.300
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
The very same car

00:48:30.300 --> 00:48:33.840
I bought in Charleston,
West Virginia, 22 years ago

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:37.208
is 2 and 1/2 times the cost now.

00:48:37.208 --> 00:48:38.250
JOHN STEELE GORDON: Yeah.

00:48:38.250 --> 00:48:40.925
AUDIENCE: So that's
inflation, isn't it?

00:48:40.925 --> 00:48:43.300
JOHN STEELE GORDON: No, you
have a much better car today.

00:48:43.300 --> 00:48:46.572
AUDIENCE: Well, no, I
mean, how is it any better?

00:48:46.572 --> 00:48:48.030
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
Well, they last

00:48:48.030 --> 00:48:49.990
a lot longer than they used to.

00:48:49.990 --> 00:48:52.770
They have all kinds of
wonderful gadgets, like GPS.

00:48:52.770 --> 00:48:55.920
I noticed when I got
in the car this morning

00:48:55.920 --> 00:48:58.600
to get to the
airport, and the car,

00:48:58.600 --> 00:49:01.798
it automatically changed the
clock to Daylight Saving Time.

00:49:01.798 --> 00:49:03.840
And I thought, that's
really neat because I never

00:49:03.840 --> 00:49:06.420
remember to do that.

00:49:06.420 --> 00:49:13.050
And I mean, cars are pretty
mature technology basically.

00:49:13.050 --> 00:49:17.340
But I bought my first
computer in 1986.

00:49:17.340 --> 00:49:21.870
It had a 12-inch black and
white cathode ray monitor, a dot

00:49:21.870 --> 00:49:24.270
matrix printer, which
was terribly noisy

00:49:24.270 --> 00:49:27.420
and only did one typeface.

00:49:27.420 --> 00:49:32.740
I had 8 mgs of RAM and
20-megabyte hard drive,

00:49:32.740 --> 00:49:34.950
which I thought in 1986,
that was pretty sexy.

00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:42.390
In 2019 terms, in dollars,
it cost around $7,000.

00:49:42.390 --> 00:49:47.730
Today, I have a 24-inch high
def, flat screen, full color

00:49:47.730 --> 00:49:49.380
monitor.

00:49:49.380 --> 00:49:52.980
I have an inkjet printer.

00:49:52.980 --> 00:49:57.930
I have 8 gigabytes of RAM, and
I have a terabyte of hard drive

00:49:57.930 --> 00:50:02.460
that's 50,000 times as big as
my first hard drive 30-odd years

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:03.400
ago.

00:50:03.400 --> 00:50:08.870
And, of course, the whole
system cost around $1,200.

00:50:08.870 --> 00:50:11.560
So that's a technology
that's rapidly advancing,

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:14.440
and so the prices are
rapidly collapsing,

00:50:14.440 --> 00:50:16.340
whereas automobiles are--

00:50:16.340 --> 00:50:20.062
they've been around
since 1896 when--

00:50:20.062 --> 00:50:23.827
who was it-- Mercedes--

00:50:23.827 --> 00:50:24.910
Mercedes was the daughter.

00:50:24.910 --> 00:50:28.630
I forget the man's name who
invented the carburetor, which

00:50:28.630 --> 00:50:32.410
was the last little gizmo
needed to make the car work.

00:50:32.410 --> 00:50:36.060
Carburetors, of course,
have long disappeared.

00:50:36.060 --> 00:50:40.170
So I think that's
mostly it is we just

00:50:40.170 --> 00:50:42.458
get better and cheaper
in making these things,

00:50:42.458 --> 00:50:43.750
and they're better things, too.

00:50:43.750 --> 00:50:47.010
I mean, it used to be a car
that lasted 100,000 miles, even

00:50:47.010 --> 00:50:49.710
if you took good care
of it, that was good.

00:50:49.710 --> 00:50:53.596
Now, 200,000 miles is,
by no means, unheard of.

00:50:53.596 --> 00:50:56.030
SPEAKER: We have time--

00:50:56.030 --> 00:50:58.190
we have time for
one more question.

00:50:58.190 --> 00:51:00.850
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I'm
curious if you identify

00:51:00.850 --> 00:51:02.620
any inconsistency
or incongruence

00:51:02.620 --> 00:51:06.520
between the right's simultaneous
rejection of Socialism

00:51:06.520 --> 00:51:10.660
or the far left with their
embrace of protectionist

00:51:10.660 --> 00:51:13.410
policies at the same time,
because a lot of people

00:51:13.410 --> 00:51:15.610
might classify those as
the type of rent-seeking

00:51:15.610 --> 00:51:20.060
that you were just
mentioning a few minutes ago.

00:51:20.060 --> 00:51:22.040
JOHN STEELE GORDON:
All manufacturers

00:51:22.040 --> 00:51:26.300
think that their suppliers
should be highly competitive,

00:51:26.300 --> 00:51:28.280
but their customers
should have no choices.

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:29.684
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:31.090 --> 00:51:32.770
Again, welcome to human nature.

00:51:32.770 --> 00:51:38.560
I mean, so protection is
a bad idea just of itself.

00:51:38.560 --> 00:51:42.040
But there-- you're always
going to have tariffs

00:51:42.040 --> 00:51:43.540
because, for some
reason, sometimes

00:51:43.540 --> 00:51:47.322
we have strategic reasons
for having tariffs.

00:51:47.322 --> 00:51:50.478
I mean, we have to
have an airplane

00:51:50.478 --> 00:51:51.520
industry in this country.

00:51:51.520 --> 00:51:53.853
You can't be a great power
without an airplane industry.

00:51:56.300 --> 00:51:59.390
But it also-- you can use
it as a political tactic,

00:51:59.390 --> 00:52:04.040
as Trump is using
against China right now.

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:07.020
But high tariffs never work.

00:52:07.020 --> 00:52:10.850
Well, they-- because,
again, a tariff is a tax,

00:52:10.850 --> 00:52:12.860
so it drives up the
price for everybody else.

00:52:12.860 --> 00:52:15.560
And it allows manufacturers
in this country

00:52:15.560 --> 00:52:16.880
to be less efficient.

00:52:16.880 --> 00:52:18.800
When they're hidden
behind tariff walls,

00:52:18.800 --> 00:52:20.510
they don't have to work hard.

00:52:20.510 --> 00:52:22.280
And nobody works any
harder than they have

00:52:22.280 --> 00:52:24.780
to to accomplish their goals.

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:28.564
You know, lions don't chase
the fast zebras to gets supper.

00:52:28.564 --> 00:52:29.492
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:30.420 --> 00:52:33.846
They chase the slow ones,
who taste just as good.

00:52:33.846 --> 00:52:34.718
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:37.311
I have it on the best authority.

00:52:37.311 --> 00:52:38.193
[CHUCKLES]

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:40.230
All right.

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:41.630
Thank you very much.

00:52:41.630 --> 00:52:44.980
[APPLAUSE]

